{
  "configuration_id": "config_009",
  "reason": "With no prior evidence, the most informative first experiment should establish a strong multimodal reference point while keeping cost manageable. config_009 (multimodal, singleprofile, scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l, concat) tests whether combining a learned RNA encoder (scvi_n200) with a strong pretrained image encoder (ViT-L) via simple concatenation yields a competitive baseline. This clarifies: (1) whether multimodal fusion provides meaningful gains over what unimodal models would achieve (to be confirmed in a later experiment), (2) the performance ceiling for a single-profile setup, and (3) a reference accuracy to judge whether more complex aggregation (transformer/avgpool) or multiprofile strategies are worth the extra cost. I chose ViT-L over ResNet50 because for cellular image profiling, a large pretrained ViT is likely the dominant image feature source, and establishing this upper bound early guides whether cheaper image encoders are sufficient. Expected relative cost: medium-high (ViT-L is the primary cost driver; no transformer aggregation or multiprofile overhead, so it is cheaper than configs like 011/012/025 but more expensive than ResNet50-based configs like 001/010)."
}

